.SH NAME
armybox \- a memory-safe, #[no_std] multi-call binary providing 303 Unix utilities
.\"
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B armybox
.RB [ \-\-help | \-\-version | \-\-list | \-\-install
.RI [ \-s ]
.IR DIR ]
.br
.B armybox
.I APPLET
.RI [ ARGUMENTS .\|.\|.]
.br
.I APPLET
.RI [ ARGUMENTS .\|.\|.]
.\"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B armybox
combines 303 standard Unix utilities into a single, compact, statically linked
binary.
It is written entirely in Rust with
.B #[no_std]
(no standard library dependency), using only
.BR libc (7)
for system calls and the
.B alloc
crate for heap allocation.
.PP
The resulting binary is approximately 500\ KB and can serve as the sole
userland for an embedded Linux system or a minimal container image.
.B armybox
is designed as a drop-in replacement for BusyBox and Toybox, with a focus
on memory safety and POSIX.1-2017 compliance.
.PP
Like BusyBox,
.B armybox
is a \(lqmulti-call binary\(rq: a single executable that behaves differently
depending on the name by which it is invoked.
Typically, a directory of symbolic links is created, each named after an
applet and pointing to the
.B armybox
binary.
.\"
.SH INVOCATION
.B armybox
determines which applet to run in two ways:
.TP
.B Symlink invocation
If the basename of
.BR argv [0]
matches a known applet name, that applet is executed.
For example, if
.I /bin/ls
is a symlink to
.IR /bin/armybox ,
running
.B /bin/ls \-la
invokes the
.B ls
applet with the
.B \-la
argument.
.TP
.B Direct invocation
When invoked as
.BR armybox ,
the first non-option argument is treated as the applet name.
For example:
.RS
.nf
.B armybox ls \-la
.B armybox sh
.B armybox tar xf archive.tar.gz
.fi
.RE
.\"
.SH OPTIONS
When invoked as
.BR armybox :
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display a brief usage message and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Display the version number and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-list
Print the names of all available applets, one per line, and exit.
Useful for scripting:
.RS
.nf
.B armybox \-\-list | sort
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI \-\-install " DIR"
Create hard links in
.I DIR
for every applet.
The
.B armybox
binary must be accessible from the target directory.
.TP
.BI "\-\-install \-s " DIR
Create symbolic links instead of hard links.
This is the recommended method:
.RS
.nf
.B armybox \-\-install \-s /usr/bin
.fi
.RE
.\"
