.SH INSTALLATION
.SS Building from Source
.nf
.B "rustup target add x86_64-unknown-linux-musl"
.B "CC=musl-gcc cargo build \-\-release \-\-target x86_64-unknown-linux-musl"
.fi
.SS Installing Symlinks
After placing the
.B armybox
binary in a suitable location (e.g.\&
.IR /bin/armybox ),
create symlinks for all applets:
.PP
.nf
.B "armybox \-\-install \-s /bin"
.B "armybox \-\-install \-s /sbin"
.B "armybox \-\-install \-s /usr/bin"
.B "armybox \-\-install \-s /usr/sbin"
.fi
.\"
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOME
Used by various applets for the user's home directory.
.TP
.B PATH
Used by the shell and applets that search for executables.
.TP
.B PS1
Primary prompt string for the shell.
.TP
.B PS2
Secondary prompt string for the shell.
.TP
.B IFS
Internal field separator used by the shell for word splitting.
.TP
.B TERM
Terminal type; used by
.BR vi ,
.BR clear ,
.BR reset ,
and other terminal-aware applets.
.TP
.B TZ
Timezone; used by
.BR date ,
.BR hwclock ,
and other time-related applets.
.\"
.SH EXIT STATUS
Each applet follows the POSIX.1-2017 exit status conventions:
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
General errors (file not found, permission denied, etc.).
.TP
.B 2
Incorrect usage (invalid options, missing operands).
.PP
Some applets define additional exit codes as specified by their respective
POSIX standards.
.\"
.SH CONFORMING TO
.B armybox
aims for full compliance with IEEE Std 1003.1-2017 (POSIX.1-2017) for all
utilities defined by the standard.
See
.UR https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799/
POSIX.1-2017
.UE .
.\"
.SH NOTES
.B armybox
is implemented in Rust with
.BR #[no_std] ,
meaning it does not link against the Rust standard library.
This results in an extremely small binary suitable for:
.IP \(bu 2
Embedded Linux systems
.IP \(bu 2
Docker
.B FROM scratch
images
.IP \(bu 2
Initramfs environments
.IP \(bu 2
Recovery systems
.IP \(bu 2
IoT devices
.PP
The entire binary is typically under 500\ KB when statically linked against musl.
.\"
